Question:
Evaluate the limit as x approaches infinity and negative infinity of the function:

{eq}f(x)= \sqrt \frac {x^4-6x^3+6}{3x^2-10}

{/eq}

Solution:
(i) As x approaches infinity:

{eq}\\\\\begin{align*} \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty } \sqrt\frac{x^{4}-6x^{3}+6}{3x^{2}-10} & = \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty } \sqrt\frac{1-\frac{6}{x}+\frac{6}{x^{4}}}{\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\frac{10}{x^{4}}} \ \ \ \ \ \texttt{(Dividing numerator and the denominator by }x^{4})

\\\\& = \lim_{x\rightarrow \infty } \sqrt\frac{1}{0}

\\\\& = \infty

\\\\\end{align*}

{/eq}

Therefore, the limit of the function as x approaches infinity is infinity.


(ii) As x approaches negative infinity:

{eq}\\\\\begin{align*} \lim_{x\rightarrow -\infty } \sqrt\frac{x^{4}-6x^{3}+6}{3x^{2}-10} & = \lim_{x\rightarrow -\infty } \sqrt\frac{1-\frac{6}{x}+\frac{6}{x^{4}}}{\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\frac{10}{x^{4}}} \ \ \ \ \ \texttt{(Dividing numerator and the denominator by }x^{4})

\\\\& = \lim_{x\rightarrow -\infty } \sqrt\frac{1}{0}

\\\\& = \infty

\\\\\end{align*}

{/eq}

Therefore, the limit of the function as x approaches negative infinity is also infinity.

